#use(std/code)
#use(std/rfc2119)

#section{RFC 2119 Keywords}::

#code{#use(std/rfc2119)} pre-declares the RFC 2119 / RFC 8174 requirement
keywords as cross-references built on #code{std/xref}: #code{#ref} them by
lowercase-kebab id, list them with backlinks via #code{#glossary}, and emit
the standard boilerplate paragraph with #code{#rfc2119-boilerplate}

#code(lang: "plain")
	#rfc2119-boilerplate

	The server #ref('must) reject expired tokens and #ref('shall-not) reuse a
	nonce. It #ref('should) rotate keys; caching is #ref('optional).

	#glossary

Live:

#rfc2119-boilerplate

The server #ref('must) reject expired tokens and #ref('shall-not) reuse a
nonce. It #ref('should) rotate keys; caching is #ref('optional)

#glossary

(Shared-info note: in this how-to set the glossary also lists the
#code{virtual machine} def from the cross-references page.)

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		The eleven spellings collapse to #strong{five} requirement levels; synonyms (#code{required}, #code{shall},
		#code{recommended}, #code{optional}, …) are aliases — #code{#ref('shall-not)} renders "SHALL NOT" but links to,
		and is backlinked under, the canonical MUST NOT entry
	#item: Ids are host code: quote them (#code{#ref('must)} or #code{#ref("must")})
	#item
		Without #code{#rfc2119-boilerplate} the keywords are glossary-only — refs render and the glossary lists them, but
		the #code{#def-<id>} fragments have no in-page target. Emit the boilerplate once, near the top
	#item
		Registration is idempotent per document and emits no elements by itself — a #code{#use(std/rfc2119)} that refs
		nothing leaves no trace in the output
